          "detail": "Claims E18/E25 (EPI) and E24 (Sutton Trust) are cited only via advocacy aggregators (faithschoolersanonymous.uk, fairadmissions.org.uk). The rationale treats these as 'independent corroboration' of the Humanists UK advocacy finding, but the underlying EPI and Sutton Trust reports are not directly cited evidence units \u2014 the verdict is relying on advocacy-mediated summaries of those findings. This does not reach blocking level because the rationale does flag the advocacy source issue and the quotes are consistent with the stated claims, but the independence claim is overstated."
        },
        {
          "check": "tier_integrity",
          "severity": "warning",
          "detail": "E21 (2017 Accord Coalition-commissioned poll) is tiered 'measurable'. Poll results are factual data, but a poll commissioned by an advocacy group with undisclosed methodology, now seven years old, sits awkwardly as 'measurable' \u2014 it would be safer as 'projected' (attitudinal estimate) or at minimum flagged as advocacy-commissioned in the claim itself. The claim text does name the commissioning body, which partially mitigates this."
        }
      ],
      "notes": "The verdict is broadly sound: the direction of 'worsens' is supported by multiple claims (EPI, Sutton Trust via advocacy summaries, Humanists UK, Accord Coalition, CRAE) all pointing toward increased segregation, with a genuine government counter-claim included. Tier discipline is mostly correct \u2014 Humanists UK is appropriately marked projected. Magnitude 'minor' is defensible given the community-cohesion duty remaining and uncertain uptake. No motive attribution, no lever leakage, no false balance issue. The two warnings above do not block publication but should prompt the author to either obtain direct EPI/Sutton Trust citations or qualify the 'independent' characterisation in the rationale."
    },
